4 estimated that roughly 70% of U.S. adults are in favor of the death penalty for a person convicted of murder. A random sample of 750 U.S. adults is chosen. Let X be the number of adults (out of 750) who favor the death penalty.","position":{"start":{"line":51,"column":1},"end":{"line":51,"column":1}},"key":"U87Ad0ESiC"}],"key":"Ei9z7hWhni"},{"type":"anywidget","esm":"/textbook/build/quiz-widget-a5666538464903eb44b37d3c2138adaa.mjs","model":{"question":"What are the mean and standard deviation of X?","choices":[{"correct":true,"text":"Mean 525, standard deviation about 12.5","feedback":"Correct! $\\mu = 525$ and $\\sigma = \\sqrt{157.5} \\approx 12.5$."},{"correct":false,"text":"Mean 525, standard deviation 157.5","feedback":"157.5 is the variance—take its square root for the standard deviation."},{"correct":false,"text":"Mean 375, standard deviation about 12.5","feedback":"The mean is np = $750 \\times 0.7 = 525$, not 375."}],"multi":false,"hint":"$\\mu$ = np = 750(0.7); $\\sigma = \\sqrt{np(1-p)} = \\sqrt{750 \\times 0.7 \\times 0.3}$.","explanation":""},"key":"Qjza6F986M"},{"type":"anywidget","esm":"/textbook/build/quiz-widget-a5666538464903eb44b37d3c2138adaa.mjs","model":{"question":"Using the 2-standard-deviations criterion, would it be unusual for only 350 of the 750 sampled adults to favor the death penalty?","choices":[{"correct":true,"text":"Yes—350 is far outside the range 500 to 550","feedback":"Correct! 350 is far below 500 (in fact, 14 standard deviations below the mean)—extremely unusual, and it would make us doubt the claimed 70%."},{"correct":false,"text":"No—350 is within 2 standard deviations of the mean","feedback":"350 is nowhere near the ordinary range of 500 to 550."}],"multi":false,"hint":"The ordinary range is $525 \\pm 2(12.5)$, i.e., 500 to 550.","explanation":""},"key":"QoNUJCEpJQ"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":71,"column":1},"end":{"line":71,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The Shape of Binomial Distributions","position":{"start":{"line":71,"column":1},"end":{"line":71,"column":1}},"key":"n4R6TNjoRU"}],"identifier":"the-shape-of-binomial-distributions","label":"The Shape of Binomial Distributions","html_id":"the-shape-of-binomial-distributions","implicit":true,"key":"U9zPR9G7yN"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":73,"column":1},"end":{"line":73,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Before we move on to continuous random variables, let’s investigate the shape of binomial distributions. For different values of p, binomial distributions can be symmetric, skewed right, or skewed left:","position":{"start":{"line":73,"column":1},"end":{"line":73,"column":1}},"key":"iccy6b5D4H"}],"key":"vacI54JA6a"},{"type":"container","kind":"figure","children":[{"type":"image","url":"/textbook/build/m11-binomial-shapes-17471cadb0d50543a7c1d83e0cfc045e.svg","alt":"Three probability histograms for binomial distributions with n equal to 10. For p equal to 0.1 the distribution is skewed right, with the probability piled up near 0. For p equal to 0.5 it is symmetric, centered at 5. For p equal to 0.9 it is skewed left, with the probability piled up near 10.","key":"d23mqWUdeu","urlSource":"images/gen/m11-binomial-shapes.svg"}],"enumerator":"1","key":"WXicWke7Xc"},{"type":"anywidget","esm":"/textbook/build/quiz-widget-a5666538464903eb44b37d3c2138adaa.mjs","model":{"question":"A binomial distribution has $n = 20$ and $p = 0.05$. What shape would you expect its probability histogram to have?","choices":[{"correct":true,"text":"Skewed right","feedback":"Correct! With p much smaller than 0.5, most of the probability is on small values of X, leaving a long right tail—skewed right."},{"correct":false,"text":"Skewed left","feedback":"Skewed left occurs when p is large (close to 1)."},{"correct":false,"text":"Symmetric","feedback":"Symmetry occurs when $p = 0.5$ (or when n is very large)."}],"multi":false,"hint":"With small p, successes are rare—where does the probability pile up?","explanation":""},"key":"LHlU1oL7t9"}],"key":"Ws8tUUfB5E"}],"key":"tmzJZ7UqDP"},"references":{"cite":{"order":[],"data":{}}},"footer":{"navigation":{"prev":{"title":"The Binomial Formula in Action","url":"/pages/p146-m11-binomial-random-variables-3-of-4","group":"Section 8: Random Variables"},"next":{"title":"Continuous Random Variables: An Introduction","url":"/pages/p148-m11-continuous-random-variables","group":"Section 8: Random Variables"}}},"domain":"http://localhost:3000"}